📄 Titanic Survival Prediction – Project Report
🔹 1. Objective

The objective of this project is to build a predictive model that can determine whether a passenger survived the Titanic disaster based on available features such as age, gender, passenger class, and other attributes. The task focuses on applying data preprocessing, machine learning modeling, and evaluation techniques using Python and Scikit-learn.

🔹 2. Dataset Description

The dataset used for this project is the Titanic Dataset Kaggle, which contains passenger-level information.

Key Features:
Survived (Target Variable): 0 = No, 1 = Yes
Pclass: Passenger class (1st, 2nd, 3rd)
Sex: Gender of passenger
Age: Age in years
SibSp: Number of siblings/spouses aboard
Parch: Number of parents/children aboard
Fare: Ticket fare
Embarked: Port of embarkation
🔹 3. Approach and Methodology
Step 1: Data Loading and Exploration

The dataset was loaded using Pandas and explored using functions such as .head(), .info(), and .describe(). Missing values and data types were identified during this stage.

Step 2: Data Preprocessing
Handling Missing Values:

Missing values in the Age column were replaced using the median value to handle skewness and outliers.
Missing values in the Embarked column were filled using the mode (most frequent value).
Dropping Irrelevant Columns:
Columns such as Name, Ticket, Cabin, and PassengerId were removed as they do not contribute significantly to prediction or contain excessive missing values.
Encoding Categorical Variables:
Categorical features like Sex and Embarked were converted into numerical format using label encoding.
Step 3: Feature Selection
The target variable was Survived.
All remaining columns were used as input features.
Step 4: Train-Test Split

The dataset was divided into training and testing sets using an 80:20 ratio to ensure proper model evaluation on unseen data.

Step 5: Model Building

A classification model was built using Logistic Regression.

This model was selected because:

It is suitable for binary classification problems
It is simple, interpretable, and efficient for beginner-level datasets
Step 6: Model Evaluation

The model performance was evaluated using:

Accuracy Score
Confusion Matrix
Classification Report (Precision, Recall, F1-score)
🔹 4. Model Performance
The model achieved an accuracy between 75% and 85% (depending on dataset variation).
The confusion matrix showed a balanced prediction between survivors and non-survivors.
Precision and recall values indicated that the model performs reasonably well in identifying survival outcomes.
🔹 5. Observations
Gender (Sex) had a strong influence on survival; female passengers had a higher survival rate.
Passenger Class (Pclass) significantly impacted survival; first-class passengers had better chances.
Age showed moderate influence, with children having slightly higher survival rates.
Features like Fare and family-related variables also contributed to predictions.
🔹 6. Conclusion

The Logistic Regression model successfully demonstrated how machine learning can be applied to predict outcomes based on structured data. The model achieved good accuracy and provided meaningful insights into survival patterns.

Although the model is relatively simple, it highlights the importance of data preprocessing, feature selection, and evaluation in building effective predictive systems. Future improvements can include using advanced models such as Decision Trees or Random Forests to enhance performance.

🔹 7. Future Improvements
Use advanced algorithms like Random Forest or Gradient Boosting
Perform feature engineering (e.g., family size, title extraction)
Apply cross-validation for more robust evaluation
Tune hyperparameters to improve accuracy
